// The global substance registry — the numeric half of the material library.
//
// The Blender side is `materials.blend` next to this file: one material datablock per entry,
// LINKED (never appended) into each tank's .blend and assigned to its ballistic meshes. The
// datablock NAME is the join key; this file owns every scalar (design doc §12: no numbers in
// the model, §13.7: substance is authored per-plate, paint is a runtime livery).
//
// Every entry is one of three KINDS. `Hard(..)` is matter (ADR-0040): a layer of the §13 walk that
// resists the shell, is charged its spend and shadows the spall field. `Witness` is not matter: it
// states no resistance (none, not zero), never enters the walk, and takes dose from what the hard
// world sends through it — the shell's line at the ROUND's crew line rate (`wound`, a round file's
// field), each spall direction at its strength. A witness substance sits only on a crew volume and a
// crew volume wears only a witness substance; the bake refuses a tank file that breaks either.
// `Unworn(..)` is worn by no mesh: it states only facts a round reads and nothing the walk reads,
// so the walk refuses a volume that names it.
//
// `burst` = a burst core's facts, the two a round's burst block reads when it names this entry as
// its `core_substance` (`density`, kg/m³, and `speed_constant_mps`, the speed constant K in m/s,
// the sideways speed scale of the burst's v_s). An optional field: a `Hard(..)` entry may state it
// beside its resistance, and an `Unworn(..)` entry states it alone.
//
// `resistance` = how the substance resists a round's force law. Exactly one entry is `Reference`:
// the reference steel, whose `facts` are the force law's reference constants and one metre of which
// is 1000 reference-mm by definition. Every other entry declares `Effective(factor)`: reference-mm
// per metre of chord (the §13.2 field value), i.e. it resists as `factor / 1000` of the reference
// force at every speed. Declared, never inferred from facts or from a fill fraction.
//
// `facts` = the material facts of a structural metal (density kg/m³, flow strength Pa at large
// strain, Young's modulus Pa, Poisson's ratio). `None` where no row is sourced; non-metals
// (ammunition, rubber, engine block) never carry facts. Facts on an `Effective` entry are recorded
// for the mechanisms that will read them; the force law does not read them yet, and the strength
// ratio they give (DERIVED, at the strength-limited end) differs from the declared factor:
//   - GunSteel, 4340-class quenched-and-tempered analogue: a / a_ref ≈ 1.129 (≈ 1129), ρ / ρ_ref ≈ 0.994.
//   - MildSteel, 1020 analogue: a / a_ref ≈ 0.460 (≈ 460), ρ / ρ_ref ≈ 1.006.
//   - TungstenCarbide, 6 % cobalt cemented-carbide analogue: a / a_ref ≈ 4.16 (≈ 4160),
//     ρ / ρ_ref ≈ 1.898.
//   Cast armour has no sourced row.
//
// `bulk` = the bulk facts of a non-metal (density kg/m³, mode I fracture toughness K_IC Pa·√m): the
// building bake reads the density for each chunk's mass; no law reads the toughness yet. Optional,
// absent read as `None`, and never stated beside `facts` (each states a density). A non-metal's
// effective factor is its compressive (crushing) strength over the reference flow strength
// (~1 GPa), × 1000: a strength ratio, the reading the steels' DERIVED rows note, not a measured
// penetration equivalence.
//
// `debris_half_angle_deg` = the half-angle α (degrees) of the spall cone a body exit through this
// substance throws, the rim of its debris field: a constant of the material, not of the exit's
// speed. Converted once at parse to the field law's ratio λ = 1/sin α (design doc §5); refused
// outside (0°, 90°].
//
// PROVENANCE: every number is authored `(value, Kind("source or reason"))`, Kind one of Measured,
// Derived, Tuned, Resolution, Unknown (`src/provenance.rs`); a number without one does not load.
// "Q2" below is the research table in
// `.agents/scratch/damage-cam-2026-10-08/research/hardness-anchor-free.md`. "Research pass
// 2026-08-07" is the Tiger metallurgy pass whose sources live in that session's record, not in the
// repo. `paintable` is a livery flag and the reference's resistance the unit's definition; neither
// is a number with a provenance.
//
// Effective factors: baseline carried from the per-node values in tiger_1.tank.ron (2026-08-06); revised by the
// material research pass 2026-08-07 (Tiger metallurgy, sources in the session record):
// DOCTRINE (Yan ruling 2026-08-07): factors are PHYSICS-HONEST, never bent for gameplay.
// Feel belongs to the laws and the hp/damage model, not to material constants. (Sibling of the
// VFX honesty doctrine.)
// THE PRINCIPLE (research pass 2026-08-07): a factor tracks ALLOY AND HEAT TREATMENT, never the
// FORMING PROCESS. "It was cast" says nothing ballistic on its own — what resists a shell is what
// the steel is made of and what was done to it afterwards. Casting, rolling and forging only matter
// insofar as they correlate with those two, and on German wartime steels they correlate WEAKLY:
// the same process spans armor and machinery grades. So a new substance is earned by a different
// alloy/treatment, not by a different way of shaping it.
//   - Tiger armor = rolled homogeneous nickel-steel, NOT face-hardened -> RHA everywhere, no
//     FaceHardened substance needed for this tank.
//   - `Cast` = 900 — cast ARMOR (Panzerguß) specifically, NOT "anything cast". German wartime
//     practice names `Panzer-Stahlguß` (armor-grade cast steel, alloyed and heat-treated to an
//     acceptance spec) and plain `Stahlguß` (ordinary cast steel, machinery) as DISTINCT GRADES
//     with distinct acceptance requirements — Spielberger reproduces the acceptance specs, and they
//     are not the same document. Eliding the two promotes every machinery casting on the vehicle to
//     armor, which is how a cast gearbox housing ends up resisting like a mantlet. Cast armor ran
//     ~5-15% below rolled ballistically; 900 is the mid of that range. If it is cast and it is NOT
//     armor-grade, it is `MildSteel` — see the note there.
//   - `GunSteel` unified at 1000 (was 800): forged autofrettaged gun steel >= RHA quality;
//     the old barrel-1000/breech-800 split was unresearched gameplay residue, and gun-part
//     fragility is the hp/damage model's job. The breech hardened 800->1000 when the material
//     bind landed (2026-08-07) — this table is what it reads now.
//   - `MildSteel` = 800: NON-ARMOUR structural and machinery steel — WROUGHT OR CAST. The name is
//     the one weak spot in this table: it reads like a forming process and is not one. It covers
//     the stamped/welded wheel bodies and forged rims, and equally the CAST running gear (sprockets
//     and idlers, 2026-08-07) — a sprocket is plain `Stahlguß`, machinery steel that happens to be
//     cast, so it belongs here and NOT in `Cast` (see the principle above). At ~0.8x RHA.
//     The wheels softened 1000->800 when the material bind landed; running-gear shots were a real
//     weak axis. Rename to `Structural` if the name ever misleads someone into the `Cast` mistake
//     this note exists to prevent.
//   - `EngineBlock` = 150: homogenized box — grey cast iron ~0.4x RHA, ~30-40% metal fraction
//     along a shot line -> 0.4 x 0.35 ~= 0.14. HL210 fork (first ~250 Tigers, aluminium
//     block) would sit ~100; declare the vehicle's date to settle it. Transmission shares
//     the substance for now (denser gear mass — split only if the sandbox ever cares).
//
// `paintable` = whether the runtime livery composes onto this substance: exterior steels yes,
// interior substances no.
//
// THIS FILE IS THE CLASSIFIER. A glTF primitive is a ballistic volume iff its material name is a
// key here, and the bake refuses one wearing an `Unworn` key; the tank RON carries no per-node
// material factor. Adding a key
// makes every mesh wearing that material armour — and removing one silently un-declares them. What
// SHOWS that is `L1.SOURCE_CENSUS`, which prints per-substance primitive counts against the
// previous committed source: evidence for a human to read, never a pass condition. No test pins
// those counts, because a second vehicle is a different model and not a broken one.
(
    substances: {
        "RHA": Hard(
            resistance: Reference,
            paintable: true,
            debris_half_angle_deg: (26.2, Derived("the constant-angle fit of the MEASURED
                normal-impact debris extents of Qi et al. 2026, shots 5 and 6 (sin α 0.446 and
                0.436): reading B of
                .agents/scratch/damage-cam-2026-10-08/research/ap-aphe-2026-10-23/shape-law.md
                §2.1–2.2, its source [4]. Those extents are 97 % contours, so the ball's bound
                may sit up to about 2° wider (§2.2, Quantile)")),
            facts: Some((
                density: (7850.0, Unknown("generic structural-steel density, carried as the force
                    law's former reference density; Q2 read no RHA-specific value (4340 MEASURED at
                    7800). Settled by an RHA datasheet density")),
                flow_strength: (1.0e9, Derived("mean of an RHA-class product's Rp0.2 900 MPa and
                    Rm 1150 MPa (MEASURED: SSAB Armox 370T Class 1 datasheet, Cimpoeru 2016 Table
                    I), the flow-stress convention of Q2; range 0.85–1.25 GPa across thickness bands")),
                youngs_modulus: (205.0e9, Tuned("chosen inside the steel range 190–210 GPa
                    (MEASURED 190 for Q&T 4340 and 1020, MakeItFrom; 210 generic); Q2 found no
                    RHA-specific value. Moves results about 1 % (Q5)")),
                poisson_ratio: (0.29, Tuned("chosen inside the steel range 0.27–0.30; Q2 found no
                    RHA-specific value. Moves results about 1 % (Q5)")),
            )),
        ),
        "GunSteel": Hard(
            resistance: Effective((1000.0, Tuned("declared effective factor: forged autofrettaged
                gun steel taken as RHA quality, unified at the reference (research pass
                2026-08-07). Not derived from the facts below, which give ≈ 1129 (DERIVED)"))),
            paintable: true,
            debris_half_angle_deg: (26.2, Unknown("no measured debris extent; RHA's value")),
            facts: Some((
                density: (7800.0, Measured("4340, MakeItFrom 'Quenched and Tempered 4340 Ni-Cr-Mo
                    Steel' (read, secondary), Q2")),
                flow_strength: (1.18e9, Derived("mean of Rp0.2 1150 MPa and Rm 1210 MPa (MEASURED,
                    one temper, MakeItFrom 4340, read, secondary), the flow-stress convention of Q2")),
                youngs_modulus: (190.0e9, Measured("Q&T 4340, MakeItFrom (read, secondary), Q2")),
                poisson_ratio: (0.29, Measured("Q&T 4340, MakeItFrom (read, secondary), Q2")),
            )),
        ),
        "Cast": Hard(
            resistance: Effective((900.0, Derived("mid of cast armour running ~5–15 % below rolled
                ballistically (research pass 2026-08-07)"))),
            paintable: true,
            debris_half_angle_deg: (26.2, Unknown("no measured debris extent; RHA's value")),
            facts: None,
        ),
        "MildSteel": Hard(
            resistance: Effective((800.0, Tuned("non-armour structural and machinery steel taken
                at ~0.8× RHA (research pass 2026-08-07). Not derived from the facts below, which
                give ≈ 460 (DERIVED)"))),
            paintable: true,
            debris_half_angle_deg: (26.2, Unknown("no measured debris extent; RHA's value")),
            facts: Some((
                density: (7900.0, Measured("1020, MakeItFrom via search excerpt (read, secondary;
                    the page redirected), Q2")),
                flow_strength: (0.40e9, Derived("from yield 240–380 MPa and Rm 430–460 MPa
                    (MEASURED, MakeItFrom 1020 via search excerpt), the flow-stress convention of
                    Q2")),
                youngs_modulus: (190.0e9, Measured("1020, MakeItFrom via search excerpt, Q2")),
                poisson_ratio: (0.29, Measured("1020, MakeItFrom via search excerpt, Q2")),
            )),
        ),
        "EngineBlock": Hard(
            resistance: Effective((150.0, Derived("grey cast iron ~0.4× RHA times a ~30–40 % metal
                fraction along a shot line ≈ 140, rounded to 150 (research pass 2026-08-07). The
                fill scaling is an assumption, not a fact"))),
            paintable: false,
            debris_half_angle_deg: (26.2, Unknown("no measured debris extent; RHA's value")),
            facts: None,
        ),
        "Ammunition": Hard(
            resistance: Effective((200.0, Unknown("no derivation recorded; carried from the
                per-node factors of the first tank file (2026-08-06). Settled by an effective
                resistance for stowed rounds and propellant"))),
            paintable: false,
            debris_half_angle_deg: (26.2, Unknown("no measured debris extent; RHA's value")),
            facts: None,
        ),
        "Rubber": Hard(
            resistance: Effective((50.0, Unknown("no derivation recorded; carried from the
                per-node factors of the first tank file (2026-08-06). Settled by a penetration
                equivalence for road-wheel rubber"))),
            paintable: false,
            debris_half_angle_deg: (26.2, Unknown("no measured debris extent; RHA's value")),
            facts: None,
        ),
        // A sub-calibre round's penetrator core (`assets/rounds/`); no armour is made of it. Its facts
        // are a modern 6 % cobalt cemented-carbide grade's, the class analogue: the wartime cores'
        // composition is not recorded here.
        "TungstenCarbide": Hard(
            resistance: Effective((1000.0, Unknown("carried at the reference factor: no armour is
                made of it and nothing reads a penetrator's factor under the rigid rule; settled
                when the core-vs-plate hardness contest reads it. Not derived from the facts below,
                which give ≈ 4160 (DERIVED)"))),
            paintable: false,
            debris_half_angle_deg: (26.2, Unknown("no measured debris extent; RHA's value")),
            facts: Some((
                density: (14900.0, Measured("14.9 g/cm³ (ASTM C20), grade ACI-Co6 (WC, 6 % cobalt,
                    submicron grain): CoorsTek, Tungsten Carbide datasheet 01129 E (2017), read")),
                flow_strength: (4.585e9, Derived("the ACI-Co6 compressive strength 4585 MPa
                    (MEASURED, ASTM C773; CoorsTek datasheet 01129 E, 2017, read) taken as the flow
                    stress: a cemented carbide fails in compression with little plastic flow, so
                    its compressive strength bounds the flow stress the cavity law reads. The
                    convention is an assumption, unlike Q2's for the steels")),
                youngs_modulus: (627.0e9, Measured("627 GPa at 20 °C (ASTM C848), grade ACI-Co6:
                    CoorsTek datasheet 01129 E (2017), read")),
                poisson_ratio: (0.21, Measured("0.21 at 20 °C (ASTM C848), grade ACI-Co6: CoorsTek
                    datasheet 01129 E (2017), read")),
            )),
        ),
        // Crew. A WITNESS, not matter: a crew station neither stops a shell nor shadows a
        // fragment, so it has no resistance to state. What reduces its HP pool is the round's
        // crew line rate along the chord the shell's disc cut through it, and each spall
        // direction's strength entering it.
        "Flesh": Witness,
        // Every tree's trunk, whatever its species (`assets/trees/wood.ron` names it): one wood,
        // since a player cannot tell one species' wood from another's. No penetration data: the
        // factor is the wood's crushing strength along the grain over the reference steel's flow
        // strength, scaled to the reference's 1000 — a strength ratio, the same reading the steels'
        // DERIVED rows note, not a measured equivalence.
        "Wood": Hard(
            resistance: Effective((40.0, Unknown("no penetration data; spruce/pine crushing
                strength along the grain ~40 MPa (birch ~55) over the reference flow strength
                ~1 GPa, × 1000; the conifers' value, the forest's wood"))),
            paintable: false,
            debris_half_angle_deg: (26.2, Unknown("no measured debris extent; RHA's value")),
            facts: None,
            bulk: Some((
                density: (448.0, Derived("red spruce specific gravity 0.40 at 12 % moisture (oven-dry
                    mass over volume at 12 %; USDA FPL Wood Handbook, FPL-GTR-190, 2010, Table 5-3a,
                    read in a third-party copy of the chapter) × 1000 kg/m³ × 1.12 for the moisture
                    mass")),
                toughness: (4.2e5, Measured("red spruce, mode I, TL system, 420 kPa·√m: USDA FPL
                    Wood Handbook, FPL-GTR-190, 2010, Table 5-10, read in a third-party copy of the
                    chapter. The handbook's mode I range is 220–550 kPa·√m")),
            )),
        ),
        // Fired clay brick: every building's masonry (`assets/buildings/<id>/<id>.building.ron`
        // names it) and the armour sandbox's wall prototype (`src/sandbox/wall.rs`). No
        // penetration data: the factor is the brick unit's compressive strength over the reference
        // steel's flow strength, scaled to the reference's 1000 — the strength ratio Wood's row
        // reads, not a measured equivalence. The unit, not the masonry assemblage (12 MPa with
        // Type N mortar): a round's line crosses brick far more than mortar.
        "Brick": Hard(
            resistance: Effective((36.5, Unknown("no penetration data; molded solid clay brick mean
                unit compressive strength 36.5 MPa (s.d. 12.6; extruded 77.9), Brick Industry
                Association Technical Note 3A (2024) Table 1, read 2026-10-30, over the reference
                flow strength ~1 GPa, × 1000"))),
            paintable: false,
            debris_half_angle_deg: (26.2, Unknown("no measured debris extent; RHA's value")),
            facts: None,
            bulk: Some((
                density: (1845.0, Measured("London stock brick, Engineering ToolBox 'Bricks -
                    Densities' (read, secondary; the wall prototype's value,
                    .agents/scratch/destruction-2026-10-30-buildings/wall-prototype/README.md).
                    Range 1765–2165 kg/m³, red facings to engineering")),
                toughness: (4.9e5, Derived("midpoint of 0.33–0.65 MPa·√m, solid bricks in
                    three-point bending, notch depth 1/6 to 4/6 (MEASURED: Xiao & Shrive, 'Masonry
                    Fracture Mechanics Three Point Bending Test of Brick', 9th Canadian Masonry
                    Symposium, Table 4, read)")),
            )),
        ),
        // The 8.8 cm Pzgr. 39's burst core, named by its burst block's `core_substance`.
        "88mm_burst_core": Unworn(
            burst: Some((
                density: (1650.0, Derived("1.65 g/cm³: shape-law.md §4")),
                speed_constant_mps: (2680.0, Derived("shape-law.md §4")),
            )),
        ),
        // The 8.8 cm Sprgr. L/4.5's filler, poured amatol 60/40 (TM53 p.445), named by its burst
        // block's `core_substance`. The research note gives neither burst fact.
        "Amatol60_40": Unworn(
            burst: Some((
                density: (1600.0, Tuned("ASSUMED poured amatol 60/40, kg/m³; no source in
                    .agents/scratch/rounds-research/kwk36-rounds-2026-10-30.md gives it")),
                speed_constant_mps: (2400.0, Tuned("ASSUMED, below the Pzgr. 39 core's 2680 m/s:
                    amatol is the weaker filler; no source in the research note gives it")),
            )),
        ),
    },
)
